I built a fixed-offset zone from a value that isn't a number, and Luxon accepted it as a perfectly good zone instead of rejecting it. I'd expect the zone to report itself as invalid, and any DateTime I create with it to be invalid too, but instead I get a valid DateTime and no error, so the bad offset just silently disappears.

```js
const { DateTime, FixedOffsetZone } = require("luxon");

const zone = new FixedOffsetZone("CDT");
console.log(zone.isValid); // true, but I expected false

const dt = DateTime.fromMillis(0, { zone });
console.log(dt.isValid, dt.invalidReason); // true undefined, but I expected false "unsupported zone"
```

reports.luxon-ff252b03cc95:
I'm splitting a day across the Europe/Berlin fall-back DST change, using the two DateTimes where the local clock reads 02:00 with different offsets. I expected three intervals: one before the repeated hour, one covering the repeated hour, and one after it. Instead `splitAt` returned only two intervals, and the first one ended at `2023-10-29T02:00:00.000+01:00`, so the repeated hour was merged into the preceding interval.

```js
const zone = "Europe/Berlin";
const dayStart = DateTime.fromISO("2023-10-29T00:00:00+02:00", { zone });
const dayEnd = DateTime.fromISO("2023-10-30T00:00:00+01:00", { zone });
const dstStart = DateTime.fromISO("2023-10-29T02:00:00+02:00", { zone });
const dstEnd = DateTime.fromISO("2023-10-29T02:00:00+01:00", { zone });

Interval.fromDateTimes(dayStart, dayEnd)
  .splitAt(dstStart, dstEnd)
  .map(i => i.toISO());
// got:
// [
//   "2023-10-29T00:00:00.000+02:00/2023-10-29T02:00:00.000+01:00",
//   "2023-10-29T02:00:00.000+01:00/2023-10-30T00:00:00.000+01:00"
// ]
```
